<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Gunnery Control Test Lab - Fire-Control Observability Logger
  Developer-only.  Belongs in testlab, NOT in the shipped mod.

  PURPOSE
  docs/FIRE_CONTROL_BEHAVIOR.md carries rows marked UNTESTED and UNKNOWN,
  mostly the "Prefer My Target ON" column.  Each asks the same question: when
  the mod sends the whole ship a preferred target with no mode restriction,
  does a group in mode X actually shoot it?  This script answers that during
  ordinary free play, so a capture can be read back and a row promoted to LIVE.

  WHY THIS IS EVENT DRIVEN AND NOT A POLLING SWEEP
  The first version of this file polled every turret once a second and logged
  mode, two raycasts, range and rotation.  It ran for ten minutes on a live
  ship, produced 7252 per-turret samples, and could not answer the question at
  all.  Three findings from that capture killed the design, all now recorded in
  the research KB, and all three are why the code below looks nothing like it:

    1. $target.lastattacker resolves to the SHIP, never to a turret.  Across the
       whole capture not one of the 14 turret component ids ever appeared.  So
       "the target got hit" could not be attributed to the turret that hit it,
       which is precisely what every open row needs.
    2. $turret.rotation is the MOUNTED BASE, not the live barrel.  Each turret
       reported exactly one distinct yaw for ten minutes, through hard
       maneuvering.  Aim direction carried zero information.
    3. check_line_of_sight with excludeself="false" reported blocked 7252 times
       out of 7252.  Only excludeself="true" carries signal, and by itself it
       cannot separate a turret that held fire from one that had no shot.

  event_object_attacked_object replaces all of it.  common.xsd:13058 documents
  param3 as [attacked component, weapon], so event.param3.{2} IS the turret
  that landed the hit - the attribution lastattacker could not give.  Shipped
  proof that param3.{2} really is the weapon and not something else:
  md/notifications.xml:1515 reads that exact slot and passes it straight into
  change_relation_on_attack weapon=, whose weapon attribute is common.xsd:21620.
  object= is the ATTACKER, not the victim: common.xsd:13058 says so and
  md/cover.xml:271-273 pairs it with event_object_attacked under the comment
  "if we either attack something or are attacked by something".

  So one HIT line now carries what the entire sweep could not: which turret
  fired, what mode it was in, and what it hit.  A Defend turret hitting the
  preferred target is a single greppable line.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE
  event_weapon_fired (common.xsd:16836) would also catch shots that MISS, and
  diffing it against the hits would give a per-turret hit rate.  It is left out:
  it fires on every trigger pull, so fourteen turrets in a sustained engagement
  would swamp the log, and the only row it would help with (NO SOLUTION) is not
  one of the open ones.  Add it if that row ever matters.

  THE CENSUS, AND WHY IT SURVIVED THE REWRITE
  Hits are positive evidence only.  The Hold fire row needs the opposite: proof
  that a turret which WAS in holdfire never appeared as an attacker.  That needs
  a denominator, so a mode census still runs, but stripped to modes alone - no
  raycasts, no rotation, no range, one line per sample rather than one per
  turret, every 10s rather than every 1s.

  It sweeps weapons.operational.list, not turrets.operational.list.  The old
  sweep used the latter and thereby skipped missile turrets entirely, which are
  a separate property list (scriptproperties.xml:542-545) - an unmeasured
  emitter that could have accounted for hits nobody could explain.  turret
  derives from weapon (scriptproperties.xml:1461-1463), so the weapons list
  covers turrets, missile turrets and main guns in one pass.

  TRANSPORT
  Same flat-scalar contract as the scenario script: the engine prepends '$' to
  every Lua string key (research KB, "Lua->MD AddUITriggeredEvent transport
  contract", live-tested 2026-08-04).  Component ids arrive as components
  because Lua converts them with ConvertStringToLuaID.

    observe_toggle {enabled = true|false}
    observe_mark   {}
    observe_state  {softtgt =, aimtgt =, prefer = true|false}

  observe_state is only sent while a Direct-control session exists.  In plain
  free play it never arrives and the log simply carries player.target, which is
  correct rather than degraded.

  VOCABULARY
  Nothing here is classified as ON SOLUTION / OUT OF RANGE / OUT OF ARC /
  MASKED.  A hit is a hit; the four condition names describe why a turret could
  not shoot, and this logger no longer measures that.

  RELOAD NOTE: any change to THIS file requires a full game restart, because
  testlab/ md is only reloadable together with the rest of the extension.
  See docs/RELOADING.md.
-->
<mdscript name="X4GunneryTestLabObserve" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="../../../../#modding/xsd/md.xsd">
  <cues>
    <cue name="ObserveRoot">
      <actions>
        <!-- Off by default.  A long play session must not flood debug.log
             because the extension happens to be installed; nothing is written
             until the owner arms it from the Test Lab menu. -->
        <set_value name="$Enabled" exact="false"/>
        <set_value name="$MarkCount" exact="0"/>
        <set_value name="$Prefer" exact="false"/>
        <!-- Seeded so the census time guard has something to compare against on
             its very first check.  Derived from player.age rather than written
             as a bare -1 so it carries the time datatype: player.age is a time
             (suffix s, scriptproperties.xml:2396) and comparing a time against
             a plain number is a type error, not a false. -->
        <set_value name="$LastSample" exact="player.age - 1s"/>
      </actions>
      <cues>

        <!-- ObserveToggle
             AddUITriggeredEvent("X4GunneryTestLabObserve", "observe_toggle",
               {enabled = true|false})
             The master switch.  Resetting ObserveArm is what actually starts
             and stops logging: arming re-evaluates ObserveArm's condition,
             which activates or deactivates both listener cues beneath it. -->
        <cue name="ObserveToggle" instantiate="true">
          <conditions>
            <event_ui_triggered screen="'X4GunneryTestLabObserve'" control="'observe_toggle'"/>
          </conditions>
          <actions>
            <set_value name="ObserveRoot.$Enabled" exact="@event.param3.$enabled == true"/>
            <debug_text text="'[X4GC TEST OBSERVE] enabled=' + ObserveRoot.$Enabled + ' t=' + player.age"/>
            <reset_cue cue="ObserveArm"/>
          </actions>
        </cue>

        <!-- ObserveMark
             AddUITriggeredEvent("X4GunneryTestLabObserve", "observe_mark")
             Stamps the interesting instant so the window is greppable
             afterwards.  The label is generated here as an incrementing
             counter rather than typed by the owner: mid-combat the whole point
             is one click, and "which mark was that" is answered by reading the
             surrounding lines.  Marks are accepted even while logging is off,
             so a mark is never silently swallowed. -->
        <cue name="ObserveMark" instantiate="true">
          <conditions>
            <event_ui_triggered screen="'X4GunneryTestLabObserve'" control="'observe_mark'"/>
          </conditions>
          <actions>
            <set_value name="ObserveRoot.$MarkCount" exact="ObserveRoot.$MarkCount + 1"/>
            <debug_text text="'[X4GC TEST MARK] label=mark_' + ObserveRoot.$MarkCount + ' enabled=' + ObserveRoot.$Enabled + ' t=' + player.age"/>
          </actions>
        </cue>

        <!-- ObserveState
             AddUITriggeredEvent("X4GunneryTestLabObserve", "observe_state",
               {softtgt =, aimtgt =, prefer = true|false})
             The only two facts MD cannot see for itself: the engine SOFT target
             (MD has player.target and no soft equivalent - scriptproperties.xml
             has no player.softtarget at all) and the mod session's
             preferAllTurrets / aimTargetID.  Pushed from Lua only while a
             Direct-control session exists.  These are what make a HIT line
             answer "did it shoot MY target" rather than merely "it shot". -->
        <cue name="ObserveState" instantiate="true">
          <conditions>
            <event_ui_triggered screen="'X4GunneryTestLabObserve'" control="'observe_state'"/>
          </conditions>
          <actions>
            <set_value name="ObserveRoot.$SoftTarget" exact="@event.param3.$softtgt"/>
            <set_value name="ObserveRoot.$AimTarget" exact="@event.param3.$aimtgt"/>
            <set_value name="ObserveRoot.$Prefer" exact="@event.param3.$prefer == true"/>
          </actions>
        </cue>

        <!-- ObserveArm
             The gate.  Its children only exist while it has fired, so switching
             logging off genuinely detaches both listeners rather than leaving
             them running behind an if.

             It also binds $Ship once, here, because event_object_attacked_object
             resolves object= when the listening cue activates.  Re-arming on
             every toggle is therefore what keeps the listener pointed at the
             ship the owner is actually flying after a ship change.  Flip the
             switch off and on again after changing ships. -->
        <cue name="ObserveArm">
          <conditions>
            <check_value value="ObserveRoot.$Enabled"/>
          </conditions>
          <actions>
            <set_value name="$Ship" exact="player.ship"/>
            <debug_text text="'[X4GC TEST OBSERVE] armed ship=' + $Ship + ' name=' + $Ship.knownname + ' t=' + player.age"/>
          </actions>
          <cues>

            <!-- ObserveHit
                 The whole point of the rewrite.  One line per hit landed BY the
                 player ship, naming the weapon that landed it.

                 event.param3.{2} is the weapon, event.param3.{1} the component
                 of the victim that was struck, event.param the victim itself,
                 event.param2 the kill method (common.xsd:13058).  param3.{2} is
                 read through @ because a hit with no weapon attribution is
                 survivable - a missing turret id is worth one line saying so,
                 not a dead cue.

                 istgt is the field the open rows turn on: it is true when the
                 thing this weapon hit is the target Direct-control was aiming
                 at.  A line with mode=weaponmode.defend, prefer=true and
                 istgt=true closes the Defend row on its own. -->
            <cue name="ObserveHit" instantiate="true">
              <conditions>
                <event_object_attacked_object object="$Ship"/>
              </conditions>
              <actions>
                <set_value name="$Weapon" exact="@event.param3.{2}"/>
                <!-- Same target precedence as Direct-control itself: the soft
                     target is what the mod aims at, and player.target is the
                     free-play fallback when no session is pushing state. -->
                <set_value name="$Aimed" exact="null"/>
                <do_if value="ObserveRoot.$SoftTarget? and ObserveRoot.$SoftTarget.exists">
                  <set_value name="$Aimed" exact="ObserveRoot.$SoftTarget"/>
                </do_if>
                <do_elseif value="player.target?">
                  <set_value name="$Aimed" exact="player.target"/>
                </do_elseif>
                <debug_text text="'[X4GC TEST HIT] t=' + player.age
                  + ' weapon=' + (if $Weapon then $Weapon else 'none')
                  + ' mode=' + (if $Weapon then $Weapon.mode else 'none')
                  + ' isturret=' + (if $Weapon then $Weapon.isclass.turret else false)
                  + ' hit=' + event.param
                  + ' hitname=' + event.param.knownname
                  + ' hitcomp=' + (if @event.param3.{1} then event.param3.{1} else 'none')
                  + ' method=' + event.param2
                  + ' aimed=' + (if $Aimed then $Aimed else 'none')
                  + ' istgt=' + ($Aimed? and event.param == $Aimed)
                  + ' prefer=' + ObserveRoot.$Prefer"/>
              </actions>
            </cue>

            <!-- ObserveCensus
                 The denominator.  Hits alone are positive evidence; proving a
                 holdfire turret never fired needs proof it was in holdfire at
                 the time, which only a periodic read gives.

                 instantiate plus checkinterval, NOT reset_cue cue="this".  An
                 earlier version combined checkinterval with a trailing
                 self-reset, which froze the game hard and wrote a 4.3 GB
                 debug.log in seconds: the reset re-satisfied the condition
                 immediately, so the cue re-fired without any game time passing
                 (every line carried the identical timestamp 247034.45).
                 instantiate lets the engine own the pacing, which is the
                 shipped idiom - see StationPeaceful_Zoom_VisibilityCheck,
                 .x4-research-cache/extracted/scripts-9.00/md/cinematiccamera.xml:2004.

                 $LastSample is belt and braces on top of that: no sample is ever
                 emitted twice for the same value of player.age, so even if a
                 future edit reintroduces a re-entrant path it cannot run away
                 again.  A freeze that needs the process killed is worth two
                 lines of paranoia.

                 10s, not 1s.  Turret modes change when the owner changes them,
                 not continuously, so this only has to be fine-grained enough to
                 bracket a mode change - and at one line per sample the whole
                 census costs less than the old sweep did in a single second. -->
            <cue name="ObserveCensus" instantiate="true" checkinterval="10s">
              <conditions>
                <check_value value="player.age gt ObserveRoot.$LastSample"/>
              </conditions>
              <actions>
                <set_value name="ObserveRoot.$LastSample" exact="player.age"/>
                <!-- weapons, not turrets: the turrets list silently omits
                     missile turrets (scriptproperties.xml:542-545) and turret
                     derives from weapon (scriptproperties.xml:1461-1463), so
                     one pass over weapons covers every emitter on the hull. -->
                <set_value name="$Census" exact="''"/>
                <do_for_each name="$Weapon" in="$Ship.weapons.operational.list">
                  <set_value name="$Census" exact="$Census + $Weapon + '=' + $Weapon.mode + ','"/>
                </do_for_each>
                <debug_text text="'[X4GC TEST CENSUS] t=' + player.age
                  + ' pilot=' + (if $Ship.pilot then $Ship.pilot.command.value else 'none')
                  + ' hardtgt=' + (if player.target then player.target else 'none')
                  + ' softtgt=' + (if ObserveRoot.$SoftTarget? and ObserveRoot.$SoftTarget then ObserveRoot.$SoftTarget else 'none')
                  + ' prefer=' + ObserveRoot.$Prefer
                  + ' modes=' + $Census"/>
              </actions>
            </cue>

          </cues>
        </cue>

      </cues>
    </cue>
  </cues>
</mdscript>
